# Shape of a call

`client.ask(state, questions, options)` takes:

- `state`: what the questions are about. Pass a string, or any value
  `std.json` can serialize.
- `questions`: a struct literal whose fields are built with `tai.noul`,
  `tai.choice` or `tai.score`. Each field name becomes the question id sent to
  the API, so ids are unique by construction.
- `options`: per-call settings, covered [below]($link.ref('per-call-options')).

It returns a `tai.Result` with the typed `answers`, token `usage` and
`model()`, the versioned id that answered. The result owns no heap memory.

# `tai.noul`: yes/no

The answer is an `f64` from 0 (no) to 1 (yes). Use `.criteria(yes, no)` to
say what each end of the scale means.

[]($code.siteAsset('snippets/noul.zig').language('zig'))

# `tai.choice`: pick one option

Pass a struct literal that maps each option to its description. Use `null`
for an option that needs no description. `tai` generates an enum from the
field names, so the `switch` below must handle every option.

[]($code.siteAsset('snippets/choice.zig').language('zig'))

The answer is a `tai.ChoiceResult` of that enum:

| Field / method | Type | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `choice` | `enum` | The highest-probability option |
| `probabilities` | `std.EnumArray(E, f64)` | Every option's probability |
| `confidence` | `f64` | How certain the model is ([Confidence](https://docs.typesafe.ai/confidence)) |
| `probability(option)` | `f64` | Shorthand for `probabilities.get(option)` |

If you already have an enum, pass the type itself. Each option is sent with a
`null` description.

[]($code.siteAsset('snippets/choice-enum.zig').language('zig'))

A choice must have between 1 and 255 options, and the compiler enforces that.

# `tai.score`: rate against levels

Pass a tuple of ordered level descriptions (2 to 10 levels), and `level()`
returns an index:

[]($code.siteAsset('snippets/score-levels.zig').language('zig'))

Or name the levels with a struct literal, and `level()` returns an enum:

[]($code.siteAsset('snippets/score-named.zig').language('zig'))

The answer is a `tai.ScoreResult`:

| Field / method | Type | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `score` | `f64` | Probability-weighted level; can land between levels (e.g. `1.05`) |
| `probabilities` | `[N]f64` | Probability of each level, by index |
| `confidence` | `f64` | How certain the model is |
| `level()` | `usize` or `enum` | The single most probable level |
| `probability(level)` | `f64` | Probability of one level |

An enum type works here too: its tag names are sent as the level descriptions.

# State and instructions

`state`, instructions and descriptions accept any value that `std.json` can
serialize, so you can pass records straight in. Refer to fields by name in
backticks:

[]($code.siteAsset('snippets/structured-state.zig').language('zig'))

A plain string is sent as text. Everything else is serialized as JSON. Values
can be comptime or runtime; only the *shape* (option names, number of levels)
has to be known at compile time.

# Reusing a set of questions

A question set is an ordinary value, so you can declare it once and ask it about
many states. `tai.Result(@TypeOf(set))` names the result type, and
`tai.Answers(@TypeOf(set))` names just the answers.

[]($code.siteAsset('snippets/reuse.zig').language('zig'))

# [Per-call options]($heading.id('per-call-options'))

The last argument to `ask` is a `tai.Client.CallOptions`:

[]($code.siteAsset('snippets/call-options.zig').language('zig'))

Pin a versioned model if you've tuned confidence thresholds against it: the
`jev-latest` alias moves when a new release ships.
